AmiNetXDuo Developer drawer
===========================

What bsdsocket.library has that the NDK does not declare, and the glue to
call it.  Nothing in here is needed to run AmiNetXDuo -- it is for writing
programs against it.

The NDK's bsdsocket interface stopped moving in 2006.  Everything in the
0..143 vector range is declared by the NDK and is reached the usual way, with
<proto/bsdsocket.h>.  This drawer covers what comes after that.


What is in here
---------------

  include/aminetxduo/     the types and the constants
  include/clib/           C prototypes
  include/inline/         gcc inline macros
  include/pragmas/        SAS/C, DICE, StormC, Maxon, Aztec pragmas
  include/proto/          the one header to include; picks the two above
  include/lvo/            assembler EQUs
  sfd/                    the SFD the generated files come from
  examples/IfNames.c      a complete program
  examples/V6Only.c       another, for the IPv6 names


Using it
--------

Put include/ on the compiler's include path and include one header:

    #include <proto/aminetxduo.h>

That brings in the prototypes, the types, and the inline glue.  It does not
replace or shadow any NDK header, so it can be included beside
<proto/bsdsocket.h> in any order.

The glue jumps through SocketBase, the same base variable bsdsocket.library
already uses.  There is no second library to open and no link library to add:

    struct Library *SocketBase;
    ...
    SocketBase = OpenLibrary("bsdsocket.library", 4);

A program that already opens bsdsocket.library has done all of it.

Standard spellings are deliberately not used.  RFC 3493 puts
if_nametoindex() in <net/if.h>, and shipping a <net/if.h> would mean include
order decides silently, per source file, whether the NDK's header or ours
wins.  The path is <aminetxduo/ifindex.h> instead, and <proto/aminetxduo.h>
finds it.


Checking that the vectors are there
-----------------------------------

A header cannot tell whether the library in memory has them.  Only
lib_Revision can:

    SocketBase = OpenLibrary("bsdsocket.library", 4);
    if (SocketBase != NULL && SocketBase->lib_Revision >= AMI_IFINDEX_MIN_REVISION)
        ... if_nametoindex() and friends are safe to call ...

Every added VECTOR carries the revision that introduced it, as an
AMI_*_MIN_REVISION constant next to it.  Do this check.  They are past the end
of every published table, so an older bsdsocket.library has MakeLibrary()'s
terminator in the slot and calling one is a guru, not a failure code.

Definitions that are only types and constants -- everything in
<aminetxduo/in6.h> -- need no such check: they name calls the NDK already
declares, and nothing was added to the vector table for them.


What it covers
--------------

RFC 3493 section 4, interface identification, in <aminetxduo/ifindex.h>.
Four new library vectors, from revision 3:

    ULONG                if_nametoindex(const char *ifname);
    char                *if_indextoname(ULONG ifindex, char *ifname);
    struct if_nameindex *if_nameindex(void);
    VOID                 if_freenameindex(struct if_nameindex *ptr);

Indices start at 1 and are the same numbers GetRouteInfo() reports in
rtm_index, so a route can be matched to an interface by comparing them.

The AF_INET6 names the NDK leaves out, in <aminetxduo/in6.h>.  No vectors --
the calls are setsockopt(), getsockopt(), socket() and getaddrinfo(), which
the NDK already declares -- so lib_Revision has nothing to say about them.
What a program wants to know is whether this build has IPv6 at all, and that
is whether socket(AF_INET6, SOCK_STREAM, 0) succeeds.

    IPPROTO_IPV6  PF_INET6  INET6_ADDRSTRLEN
    IPV6_V6ONLY  IPV6_UNICAST_HOPS  IPV6_TCLASS
    IPV6_JOIN_GROUP  IPV6_LEAVE_GROUP  struct ipv6_mreq
    IPV6_MULTICAST_IF  IPV6_MULTICAST_HOPS  IPV6_MULTICAST_LOOP
    IN6ADDR_ANY_INIT  IN6ADDR_LOOPBACK_INIT
    IN6_IS_ADDR_UNSPECIFIED / LOOPBACK / MULTICAST / LINKLOCAL /
        SITELOCAL / V4MAPPED / V4COMPAT and the MC_ scopes
    struct sockaddr_storage
    AI_ADDRCONFIG

Every one of them is #ifndef-guarded, because a program written against this
NDK could not name them and will have spelled the numbers out for itself.
Two of the option numbers have a second value in the Linux lineage; the
library accepts both, and the header says which is which.

AI_V4MAPPED is deliberately absent.  This library never synthesises
::ffff:a.b.c.d from an A record, so no value for it would be truthful.  Code
that wants both families asks for AF_UNSPEC, which returns the IPv6 results
first.  Leaving the name undefined makes that a compile error to fix rather
than a silent difference in what gets connected to.


THE sockaddr_in6 TRAP
---------------------

Worth reading before writing any IPv6 code against this NDK.

Its `struct sockaddr_in` is 4.4BSD's: sin_len at offset 0, sin_family at
offset 1.  The `struct sockaddr_in6` immediately below it in the same header
is the LINUX one, pasted in verbatim -- sin6_family at offset 0, and no
sin6_len at all.  So:

    ((struct sockaddr *)&sin6)->sa_family      /* reads the PADDING byte */

sa_family lives at offset 1, and that is where sockaddr_in6 has the pad in
front of sin6_port.  Decide the family from the length instead: 16 bytes is a
sockaddr_in with its family at offset 1, 28 bytes is a sockaddr_in6 with its
family at offset 0.  bsdsocket.library does exactly that.

Never write a length byte into a sockaddr_in6.  On this NDK that byte is
sin6_family.

struct sockaddr_storage here has no ss_family for the same reason: there is
no offset at which such a member would be right for both families, and one
that is right for only one is the trap wearing a struct member's name.  It is
128 aligned bytes to receive an address into, and the length says what
arrived.


What it does not cover
----------------------

RFC 3542 -- struct in6_pktinfo, struct icmp6_filter, the IPV6_* ancillary
options and the missing CMSG_ macros -- is not here yet.  It adds no vectors,
so it will arrive as another header beside the two above and this drawer does
not change shape when it does.  Note for whoever writes it: the NDK already
has struct cmsghdr, CMSG_DATA, CMSG_FIRSTHDR and CMSG_NXTHDR in
<sys/socket.h>; what is missing is CMSG_LEN, CMSG_SPACE, CMSG_ALIGN and the
ALIGN() its own CMSG_NXTHDR expands to.

IPv4 multicast needs nothing from here: IP_MULTICAST_IF, IP_MULTICAST_TTL,
IP_MULTICAST_LOOP, IP_ADD_MEMBERSHIP, IP_DROP_MEMBERSHIP and struct ip_mreq
are all in the NDK's <netinet/in.h>.  The IPv6 five and struct ipv6_mreq are
not, so in6.h carries them.

A word on what an IPv6 join does.  There is no MLD in this stack: joining
registers the group's 33:33 address with the interface and makes the library
accept datagrams sent to it, and puts nothing on the wire.  The link-local
groups -- ff02::fb for mDNS, ff02::1:3 for LLMNR, ff02::c for SSDP -- work on
any segment.  A switch running MLD snooping with an active querier will stop
forwarding the group, and no router will forward a wider scope here, so
link-local scope is what to rely on.

The bpf_* capture path needs nothing from here either.  BIOC*, struct
bpf_hdr, struct bpf_program, struct bpf_stat, DLT_*, the BPF_* opcodes,
BPF_WORDALIGN, FIONREAD and SIOCGIFADDR are all declared by the NDK, in
<net/bpf.h>, <sys/filio.h> and <sys/sockio.h>.

NetStackQuery() and NetStackControl(), at -0x366 and -0x36c, are what
ShowNetStatus, netstat and arp are written against.  They are not published
here.  They work, but publishing them freezes their request structures, and
that is worth deciding on rather than drifting into.


Regenerating
------------

The headers under include/clib, include/inline, include/pragmas,
include/proto and include/lvo are generated from sfd/aminetxduo_lib.sfd and
must not be edited.  In the source tree:

    tools/gen-developer.sh

sfd/aminetxduo_lib.sfd is a separate file from the NDK's
bsdsocket_lib.sfd, not a patched copy of it.  Both declare the same base
variable, so the two sets of generated headers mix, and neither has to be
re-merged when the other moves.


Building the example
--------------------

    gcc -I Developer/include -o IfNames Developer/examples/IfNames.c
    gcc -I Developer/include -o V6Only  Developer/examples/V6Only.c

IfNames prints one line per interface -- index and name -- and checks that
every name maps back to its own index.  V6Only makes an IPv6 socket, sets
IPV6_V6ONLY, binds to ::1 and prints what getsockname() gives back.
